NAME
    memory - RAM, the memory tier, and disk swap

DESCRIPTION
    RAM is the hard constraint in OpenComputers: computer.totalMemory() is a
    fixed ceiling that cannot be extended. TOS detects a memory tier from the
    installed RAM and uses it (with the boot profile) to decide which optional
    subsystems load.

    Check current usage with 'mem'; see the per-piece tier on the boot System
    Configuration screen (or 'doctor').

    Free below ~16 KB is the danger zone where any allocation can fail; the
    'normal' boot profile already skips optional subsystems when RAM is tight.

DISK SWAP ("SLOW RAM")
    TOS cannot transparently page RAM to disk, but it offers an explicit
    spill-to-disk layer for cold data, backed by /var/swap. Programs use the swap
    API (swap.store/fetch, or swap.table{ hot = N }) to offload large data and
    page it back. It is size-capped and wiped on every boot. See 'man swap'.

SEE ALSO
    man swap, mem, doctor, MANUAL.md (Section 9.3)
